  [1mTesting[0m — [38;5;153mtest/current-user.test.js[39m, run by [38;5;153mnode --test[39m, with [38;5;153m"scripts": { "test": "node --test" }[39m added to
  [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m. Zero dependencies, as you asked. The cases:

  - [38;5;153mget()[39m on empty storage returns [38;5;153mnull[39m
  - [38;5;153mset()[39m then [38;5;153mget()[39m round-trips [38;5;153muserId[39m, [38;5;153musername[39m, and a [38;5;153mloginAt[39m
  - [38;5;153mclear()[39m removes it; a following [38;5;153mget()[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m
  - corrupt JSON returns [38;5;153mnull[39m [3mand[0m removes the key
  - an unknown [38;5;153mv[39m returns [38;5;153mnull[39m [3mand[0m removes the key
  - a storage stub that throws on [38;5;153msetItem[39m does not propagate: [38;5;153mset()[39m returns normally and [38;5;153mget()[39m still yields the value
    within that page life
  - [38;5;153mlogin()[39m returns a [38;5;153muserId[39m field

  The dual-mode wrapper and the injectable storage exist precisely so these run in plain Node with no DOM, no jsdom, no
  browser.

  [1mExplicitly out of scope[0m — each of these is a real thing someone could expect from "track who logged in," so the spec
  names them as not-built rather than leaving them ambiguous: no event or audit trail; no backend and no real call to
  [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m; no authentication or authorization — the stored ID is self-asserted and must not gate access to
  anything; no logout UI; no ESLint, Prettier, or Playwright; no ES-module or bundler migration; no changes to
  [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m or [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m.

  [1mOne assumption to flag,[0m which will go in the spec as [38;5;153mAssumption:[39m rather than as fact: that per-tab lifetime is
  genuinely what you want. You said "it should persist," and [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m clears on tab close — so a user who closes
  and reopens the tab is anonymous again. If "persist" meant across browser restarts, that's [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m and it's a
  one-line change, but it's worth being sure now rather than discovering it from a bug report.
